📦 What is Optional?
Optional<T> is a container object which may or may not contain a non-null value.

Think of it like a box:

If the value is present → do something

If not → handle the absence safely

✅ Why use Optional?
Avoid null checks

Prevent NullPointerException

Promote functional-style code

Improve readability and null safety

🔧 Creating an Optional
| Method                       | Description                                                           |
| ---------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `Optional.of(value)`         | Creates Optional with **non-null value**. Throws exception if `null`. |
| `Optional.ofNullable(value)` | Allows `null` value. Returns `Optional.empty()` if `null`.            |
| `Optional.empty()`           | Creates an **empty** Optional                                         |


java
Copy
Edit
Optional<String> name1 = Optional.of("Ali");           // ✅ OK
Optional<String> name2 = Optional.ofNullable(null);    // ✅ OK
Optional<String> name3 = Optional.empty();             // ✅ Empty optional

🛠️ Accessing Values
| Method                | Description                                      |
| --------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |
| `get()`               | Returns the value (⚠️ throws exception if empty) |
| `isPresent()`         | Checks if value exists                           |
| `ifPresent(Consumer)` | Runs code only if value exists                   |
| `orElse(default)`     | Returns value or default                         |
| `orElseGet(Supplier)` | Returns value or lazily supplies default         |
| `orElseThrow()`       | Throws exception if value is missing             |


✅ Example:
java
Copy
Edit
Optional<String> name = Optional.of("Sara");

// Safe access
name.ifPresent(n -> System.out.println(n)); // Output: Sara

// Default fallback
String result = name.orElse("No Name"); // Output: Sara

// Empty fallback
Optional<String> empty = Optional.empty();
String result2 = empty.orElse("Anonymous"); // Output: Anonymous
🔄 Chaining with map() and filter()
java
Copy
Edit
Optional<String> name = Optional.of("Afham");

String upper = name
    .filter(n -> n.length() > 3)
    .map(String::toUpperCase)
    .orElse("DEFAULT");

System.out.println(upper);  // Output: AFHAM
⚠️ When NOT to use Optional:
As method parameters → prefer regular null check or overloads

In performance-critical loops

Inside entities or POJOs (can bloat memory)

✅ Summary
| Concept                       | Description                |
| ----------------------------- | -------------------------- |
| `Optional.of()`               | Non-null required          |
| `Optional.ofNullable()`       | Accepts null               |
| `isPresent()` / `ifPresent()` | Check existence            |
| `orElse()` / `orElseGet()`    | Provide fallback           |
| `map()` / `filter()`          | Functional transformations |


